>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Battery Groups · Last reviewed 2026-09-22</p>
      <h1>H7 vs H8 Battery: Size, Capacity, CCA and the Upgrade Question</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        H7 and H8 are the two large European (EN/DIN) passenger-car groups — the batteries of big sedans, SUVs
        and luxury cars. They share width and height; the entire difference is <strong>~37 mm of length</strong>, which buys
        the H8 roughly <strong>20 Ah more capacity</strong> and <strong>100–150 A more CCA</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/h7/">H7</a> (L4/LN4) is about
        <strong>315 × 175 × 190 mm</strong> with typical ratings of <strong>~70–80 Ah and ~760 CCA (EN 50342)</strong>;
        <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/h8/">H8</a> (L5/LB5) is about <strong>352 × 175 × 190 mm</strong> with
        <strong>~90–95 Ah and ~850–900 CCA</strong>. Same width and height, longer case — so the upgrade H7 → H8 is
        decided by <strong>whether the tray accepts the longer battery</strong>, and on modern cars by the battery-management
        registration that follows.
      </div>

      <h2>Side-by-side comparison</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>H7 (L4 / LN4)</th><th>H8 (L5 / LB5)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Standard</strong></td><td>EN/DIN</td><td>EN/DIN</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Dimensions</strong></td><td>~315 × 175 × 190 mm</td><td>~352 × 175 × 190 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Capacity (typical)</strong></td><td>~70–80 Ah (20h)</td><td>~90–95 Ah (20h)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>CCA (typical)</strong></td><td>~760 A (EN 50342)</td><td>~850–900 A (EN 50342)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Terminals</strong></td><td>SAE post</td><td>SAE post</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Typical vehicles</strong></td><td>Large sedans, mid SUVs</td><td>Large SUVs, luxury, light trucks</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        Cross-verified typical ranges from the World Battery Hub data section; a specific battery's datasheet wins.
        CCA figures are EN 50342 (−18°C) — compare only within one standard (see
        <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">what CCA is</a>). For the full three-way picture with H6, see
        <a href="/lead-acid/h6-vs-h7-vs-h8/">H6 vs H7 vs H8</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The physical difference: ~37 mm of length</h2>
      <p>
        Both groups share the ~175 mm width and ~190 mm height of the EN/DIN large-car family, with SAE posts in the
        standard positions. The only dimensional change is <strong>length</strong>: the H8 is roughly 37 mm (about 1.5 inches)
        longer. That extra room holds larger plates, and the larger plates are the entire source of the H8's higher
        Ah and CCA.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fit rule follows directly: <strong>an H8 drops into an H7 tray only if ~37 mm of length is spare</strong>.
        Many vehicles are offered with both groups from the factory — same tray, different hold-down position — but
        plenty are not, so the tray, not the catalogue, is the authority. The
        <a href="/lead-acid/battery-size-selection/">battery size selection</a> guide covers measuring the tray properly.
      </p>

      <h2>AGM, EFB and the start-stop question</h2>
      <p>
        H7 and H8 vehicles are disproportionately <strong>start-stop</strong> models, and start-stop changes the purchase
        completely: the car needs the <strong>technology it was designed for</strong> — usually
        <a href="/lead-acid/agm-battery/">AGM</a>, sometimes <a href="/lead-acid/efb-battery/">EFB</a> — not a plain flooded battery.
        Fitting a standard flooded unit into a start-stop car shortens its life sharply and can disable the system.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second start-stop rule is <strong>registration</strong>: many modern cars track battery age and charge history
        in the battery management system, and a new battery must be registered electronically so the car resets its
        charging strategy. This applies whether the replacement is the same group or an upgrade, and it is a
        workshop step, not an optional extra. See the <a href="/lead-acid/start-stop-battery/">start-stop battery</a> guide for
        the full picture.
      </p>

      <h2>Is the H7 → H8 upgrade worth it?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fits, same technology, more reserve</strong> — a genuine upgrade: more Ah for accessory load and longer parking endurance, more CCA for cold starts.</li>
        <li><strong>Does not fit</strong> — the question ends; an H8 in an H7 tray is a safety hazard, not an upgrade.</li>
        <li><strong>Start-stop vehicle</strong> — upgrade only within the maker-approved technology (AGM→AGM, EFB→EFB) and register the battery.</li>
        <li><strong>Downgrade H8 → H7</strong> — fits physically but drops capacity and CCA; re-check the vehicle's electrical load first.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>H7 and H8 differ in <strong>length only</strong> — ~315 mm vs ~352 mm, same width and height.</li>
          <li>The extra length buys <strong>~20 Ah and ~100–150 CCA</strong> (typical): a real but modest upgrade.</li>
          <li><strong>Tray fit decides</strong> the upgrade; output decides only whether it is worth doing.</li>
          <li>On start-stop cars, <strong>match the technology (AGM/EFB) and register the battery</strong> — this matters more than the group size.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>What procurement buyers should check</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Is the group decision locked to the vehicle's actual tray and hold-down, not to a catalogue equivalence table?</li>
        <li>Are the quoted Ah and CCA figures tied to a discharge rate and a named standard (EN 50342 vs SAE vs DIN)?</li>
        <li>For start-stop fleets: is the technology (AGM / EFB) specified, and is battery registration part of the installation procedure?</li>
        <li>Is the terminal layout and polarity verified — including position, which varies across DIN/EN variants?</li>
        <li>Do quotations across suppliers compare <a href="/procurement/battery-quotation-comparison/">like for like</a> — same group, same technology, same CCA standard?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What is the difference between H7 and H8 batteries?</h3>
      <p>Length and output. Both are EN/DIN 12V groups about 175 mm wide and 190 mm tall, but H8 is roughly 37 mm longer — about 352 mm versus about 315 mm for H7 — and typically carries about 90–95 Ah and 850–900 CCA (EN) versus about 70–80 Ah and 760 CCA for H7.</p>
      <h3>Can I replace an H7 with an H8?</h3>
      <p>Only if the tray, hold-down and cables accept the ~37 mm longer H8 case. The electrical upgrade is real — more capacity and CCA — but the physical fit decides, and on start-stop vehicles the battery management registration requirement also applies.</p>
      <h3>Do H7 and H8 batteries need AGM or EFB?</h3>
      <p>If the vehicle has start-stop, yes — it needs the battery technology it was designed for, usually AGM or EFB, and many cars require electronic registration of the new battery. Replacing with a plain flooded battery in a start-stop car shortens life and disables features.</p>
      <h3>What is the H7 equivalent in North America?</h3>
      <p>H7 is commonly cross-referenced as BCI Group 94R (L4/LN4); H8 is commonly cross-referenced as BCI Group 49 (L5). These are close physical equivalents, not universal interchangeability rules — verify dimensions, terminals, hold-down and polarity before substituting.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Cross-verified from the World Battery Hub entity database and public references; where sources differ, the
        difference is flagged rather than silently resolved.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>World Battery Hub data section — <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/h7/">H7 reference page</a> and <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/h8/">H8 reference page</a>.</li>
        <li>EN 50342 — European lead-acid starter battery standard used for the quoted CCA figures.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer datasheets (AGM and EFB, H7 and H8) — dimension, capacity and CCA cross-checks.</li>
        <li>Vehicle OEM battery specifications — group and technology requirements for start-stop models.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-22</p>
    </div>
  </section>
